{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III was the last ruler of Tripoli who ruled from 1152 until his death in 1158. He was the Count of Tripoli and the ruler of the County of Tripoli. Raymond III was a member of the Anjou dynasty \n\n*Early Life and Reign*\n\nRaymond III was born around 1115. He was the son of Pons II Count of Tripoli. He became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1127. Raymond III married Maria of Hauteville in 1136. They had three sons.\n\nRaymond III was a skilled military commander and expanded the County of Tripoli. He also strengthened the county's economy and administrative structures. Raymond III died in 1158 due to injuries sustained in a battle with the Zengid forces.\n\n*Conquest of the County of Tripoli*\n\nIn 1149 Raymond III had launched a successful campaign against the County of Tripoli which was then ruled by his cousin Raymond I. Raymond III defeated his cousin at the Battle of Inab in 1149. Raymond III then marched on Jerusalem but was unable to capture it. He returned to Tripoli and continued his military campaigns in the region.\n\nRaymond III also fought against the Zengid forces who controlled much of the region. He launched a successful campaign against the Zengids in 1151. The Zengid forces were defeated at the Battle of Azazak in 1151. Raymond III then marched on Aleppo but was unable to capture it. He returned to Tripoli and continued his military campaigns in the region.\n\n*Later Life and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1158 due to injuries sustained in a battle with the Zengid forces. He was succeeded by his son Raymond IV who became the next ruler of Tripoli. Raymond III's death marked the end of the County of Tripoli's independence. The county was eventually conquered by the Mamluk Sultanate in 1289.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.001581966644152999}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-0